"tests/test_plugin_runtime.py", "tests/test_pr_gate.py", "tests/test_pr_skill.py", "tests/test_project_dir_resolution.py", "tests/test_project_label.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py", "tests/test_reference_verifier.py", "tests/test_reflection_gate.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py", "tests/test_release_verification.py", "tests/test_repo_disable.py", "tests/test_retired_claims.py", "tests/test_review_stats.py", "tests/test_reviewer_handoff.py", "tests/test_reviewer_model_dispatch.py", "tests/test_session_critic_gate.py", "tests/test_session_start_ingest.py", "tests/test_short_plan_deferral.py", "tests/test_stale_base_probes.py", "tests/test_stats.py", "tests/test_stop_abandoned_critic.py", "tests/test_stop_block_facts.py", "tests/test_stop_gate_error_posture.py", "tests/test_stranded_branch_probes.py", "tests/test_stranded_work.py", "tests/test_subagent_stop.py", "tests/test_test_report_scope.py", "tests/test_test_status_in_progress.py", "tests/test_testament_cache_probes.py", "tests/test_tree_key_memo.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py", "tests/test_verdict_cache.py", "tests/test_verify_coverage_gate.py", "tests/test_work_unit.py", "tests/test_work_unit_probes.py"], "files_oracle": [], "findings": [{"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The clock is chosen per write site, not per comparison: the ledger's `ts` moved onto the suite clock while the native-review `dispatched_at` it is differenced against stayed on the real one", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `lib/clock.py` says the records the plugin stamps and later compares read `now()`, but the migration (and `LEFT_ON_THE_REAL_CLOCK` in tests/test_clock.py) decides each read by the module that makes it, so a stamp minted in a real-clock module and compared in a suite-clock one is split across clocks. Member found: `critic_consolidate.native_review_block` stamps `dispatched_at` with `datetime.now` (critic_consolidate.py:3785). `native_review.record` passes it to `ledger.append_native_review_event`, whose event `ts` is now `clock.now()` (ledger.py:217). `review_dispatch.event_interval_seconds` differences the two, so under the suite clock a native.review event's interval comes out negative and reads as 'not measured', silently. Its partner `native_review._seconds_since` stays real, so `duration_seconds` and the measured interval disagree. The test_clock.py reason for critic_consolidate ('review ids and dispatch ages, minted and aged in this module') is false for this stamp, which leaves the module. This is the case 'When a test injects a fixed clock, EVERY actor must share that clock' warns about: one real-clock participant. Fix: move native_review_block's `dispatched_at` and `_seconds_since` onto `clock` together, because they are a pair. Then search the class: every real-clock stamp in a LEFT_ON_THE_REAL_CLOCK file that is persisted or returned, checking whether any reader differences it against a `clock.now()` stamp. Pin one with a test asserting a native.review event's `event_interval_seconds` is measured under the suite clock.", "files": ["plugin/lib/ledger.py", "plugin/lib/clock.py", "tests/test_clock.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The one scrubbed-env helper did not reach every hook child: several tests still build a from-scratch env for `prawduct-hook`, so those children get no git isolation and no suite clock", "recommendation": "Scope: class, a material change since rev-20261123T164037Z-5f10b40c R-3, which deferred this consolidation to #1218, the work this bundle carries out. Why it broke: the conversion went by helper name, not by the shape 'a subprocess env dict literal without an os.environ spread'. tests/conftest.py now says every child runs under TEST_ENV and that a scrubbed child is built with `scrubbed_env`. Hand-built `env={\"PATH\": \"/usr/bin:/bin\", ...}` hook children remain: tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py `_cli` (in this diff, beside a `_run_hook` it did convert), tests/test_operator_verification.py (4 sites), tests/test_anchor_repair.py `_run`, tests/test_dispositions.py `_hook`, tests/test_gate_flags_fail_closed.py, and tests/test_deprecated_inert_commands.py. The git these hooks spawn reads the machine's global config, and the hook stamps the wall clock while the fixture's commits carry TEST_NOW, which is the mixed-clock state the bundle exists to remove. Each is one future assertion away from a machine-dependent or date-dependent result. Fix: convert each to `scrubbed_env` or `run_hook`. Close the class with a check, for example a test that scans tests/ for `env={` literals passed to a `prawduct-hook` subprocess and lacking `scrubbed_env`/`os.environ`, so a new copy cannot be added unseen.", "files": ["tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py", "tests/conftest.py"], "fid": "R-2"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 2, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "test_plan_mint pins the suite's UTC date against code that writes the LOCAL date, so it goes red in UTC+12..+14", "recommendation": "Instance. plan_mint writes `last_validated` from `clock.local_today()`, but tests/test_plan_mint.py:139 expects `suite_now().date()` (UTC). TEST_NOW is noon UTC on 1 January, which is already 2 January in Pacific/Auckland (NZDT, +13) and Kiribati, so the assertion fails there. Before this change both sides read `date.today()` and agreed in every zone. conftest records the UTC-11..+11 bound on purpose, so this is a note and not a finding. If a contributor or runner outside that band ever matters, compare against `clock.local_today()` in the test. Relatedly, test_clock's `test_the_suites_instant_is_the_same_day_everywhere` checks only America/Los_Angeles, so 'everywhere' claims more than the test checks.", "files": ["tests/test_plan_mint.py", "tests/test_clock.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation had nothing to reconcile: no plan claims this branch", "recommendation": "The manifest's scope is unbound ('no live plan claims `chore/testament-cache-blockers`') and record_lint read no records, so no `governed_by:` disposition exists to test against the diff. The production changes (lib/clock.py's FROZEN_ENV seam read by production code, and core._shared_ignore_matches now keeping the caller's non-location GIT_* environment) trace to the owner's 2026-11-23 #1218 ruling in .prawduct/owner-rulings.md, so this review found no undocumented architectural decision. The branch also adds no change record. coverage.py's `no-entry` check owns that and will refuse at /prawduct:pr, so no review finding is raised for it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/clock.py", "plugin/lib/core.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Stale citation: tests/_hook_inproc.py still names `GIT_TEST_ENV`, renamed to `TEST_ENV` in conftest by this bundle", "recommendation": "Instance. Comment wording that no test or gate reads. When the file is next touched, change the token to `TEST_ENV` (module docstring, the 'cwd and environment' bullet). No commit is wanted for this alone.", "files": ["tests/_hook_inproc.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`clock.now_iso()` is the record-stamp helper, but 9 migrated sites still spell `clock.now().strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")`, and evidence._now_iso / advisory_store._utcnow_iso wrap it again", "recommendation": "Class: every `now().strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")` in plugin/. The format predates this bundle, so nothing breaks today. Using `now_iso()` at those sites would make the clock module the one owner of the stamp format its docstring describes. Optional.", "files": ["plugin/lib/clock.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/advisory_store.py", "plugin/lib/ledger.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`clock.epoch()` has no production caller; only tests/test_clock.py reads it", "recommendation": "Delete it, or keep it until a caller needs it. Small dead surface either way.", "files": ["plugin/lib/clock.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "review_dispatch.py adds lib/'s first `from . import X` / `except ImportError: import X` dual import, for tools/ scripts that import it as a top-level module", "recommendation": "Works, because clock.py is stdlib-only. The alternative is to have the three tools/ scripts put plugin/ on sys.path and import `lib.review_dispatch`, which keeps lib/ single-mode. Worth doing only if a second module needs the idiom.", "files": ["plugin/lib/review_dispatch.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Import placement nits from the mechanical migration", "recommendation": "backlog/snapshot.py and pr_gate.py put `from .. import clock` / `from . import clock` straight after a stdlib import with no blank line. upstream_probes.py is left with a doubled blank line where the datetime import was removed. Cosmetic, so no edit is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/snapshot.py", "plugin/lib/pr_gate.py", "plugin/lib/upstream_probes.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The in-process runner's docstring names `GIT_TEST_ENV`, which this branch renamed to `TEST_ENV`", "recommendation": "Instance. tests/_hook_inproc.py line 20 says the overlay keeps \"`GIT_TEST_ENV` and any seam a fixture sets\", but tests/conftest.py now defines `TEST_ENV` (plus `scrubbed_env`) and `GIT_TEST_ENV` no longer exists anywhere under tests/ or plugin/ (`git grep GIT_TEST_ENV HEAD -- tests plugin` returns only this line). Change the name to `TEST_ENV` if the file is touched again. It is docstring wording that nothing reads, so it does not need its own commit.", "files": ["tests/_hook_inproc.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The pending testament-cache-areas fragment describes the `real_git_clock` stopgap that this branch removes, and this branch has no fragment of its own yet", "recommendation": ".prawduct/changes/testament-cache-areas.md is unreleased (still pending in changes/) and is outside this diff. It tells release-note readers that tests opt out with `@pytest.mark.real_git_clock` and that the isolating env is `GIT_TEST_ENV`, and it calls this a stopgap until #1218. This branch is that #1218 change: it removes both names and adds `lib/clock.py` (`PRAWDUCT_TEST_ONLY_FROZEN_NOW`). PR Step 1c (`check-change-log-entry`) will require this branch to add its own fragment. When you write it, say that the stopgap marker is gone and that git and the plugin now share one test clock, so the two fragments read correctly once they are cut into one release. The `closes:` list should include #1218. The owner's 2026-11-23 ruling in .prawduct/owner-rulings.md (item 2) already records the design decision.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/testament-cache-areas.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A frozen production clock is honored silently, with no trace when PRAWDUCT_TEST_ONLY_FROZEN_NOW is set", "recommendation": "plugin/lib/clock.py `frozen()` silently ignores a malformed value, which is fine. It also silently obeys a well-formed one, so if the variable leaked into a real session (for example, exported in a developer's shell while debugging), every evidence stamp, `.session-start` and ledger event would freeze, and the freshness gates would misjudge with nothing in the logs. That is unlikely: the name is explicit and conftest sets it only inside the pytest process. One possible step is a single stderr NOTE from the hook's SessionStart when `clock.frozen()` is not None, outside the test suite. Judgment call; no change is needed.", "files": ["plugin/lib/clock.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-10"}], "duration_seconds": 720, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `chore/testamen